Learning objectives
After this exercise, you should be able to:

formulate a regression task in terms of data, features, targets, and performance measures;
design a small CFD parameter study and separate training, validation, and test cases;
run and inspect OpenFOAM and Basilisk simulations and assess whether their output is suitable for further analysis;
train and evaluate a velocity-profile surrogate, with and without physical feature transformations;
distinguish numerical simulation error, surrogate error, and variation between training runs.

Read Topic 2 — Introduction to machine learning. The practical project combines CFD data generation with regression. The cylinder and bubble simulations introduce the tools and data checks; the channel-flow simulations provide the regression dataset. This exercise can span several practical sessions.

Learning objectives
After this exercise, you should be able to:

decompose a fluid-mechanical application into suitable learning tasks and explain how they connect;
identify the required data, propose meaningful checks, and explain which steps can use analytical or numerical methods;
set up the course tools on Ubuntu, or on Windows 11 using WSL 2 or an Ubuntu VirtualBox VM, and verify the main components.

Learning objectives
After this lecture, you should be able to:

explain why fluid mechanics and machine learning complement each other, identify common types of flow data, and describe the roles of domain knowledge, mathematics, and computer science;
distinguish regression, classification, dimensionality reduction, clustering, and reinforcement learning using fluid-mechanical examples;
identify features, targets, and samples in supervised learning, and states, actions, policies, and rewards in reinforcement learning;
explain how reduced representations can retain useful information, how information loss affects a task, and why predictive accuracy does not automatically provide physical understanding;
decompose an application into subproblems and outline how learning methods can be combined with analytical and numerical techniques;
identify challenges involving data quality, generalization, computational cost, and physical reliability, and propose meaningful checks for an application;
describe the extract–learn–use workflows and distinguish analysis, model integration, transfer across scales, and feedback loops.

Learning objectives
After this lecture, you should be able to:

interpret Mitchell’s definition of machine learning in terms of data, tasks, and performance measures, and outline the iterative workflow from problem definition to deployment;
distinguish parametric from non-parametric models and deterministic from probabilistic predictions, using representative examples;
compare regular grids, random sampling, Latin hypercube sampling, Sobol sequences, and adaptive sampling in terms of coverage, randomization, and sampling cost;
explain why normalization matters, apply min–max scaling and standardization, and relate physical scaling and floating-point precision to numerical behavior;
design physically and mathematically motivated features, check their availability in the target application, and explain common feature-selection strategies and their limitations;
formulate a supervised dataset, a neural-network mapping, predictions, and a training objective, and describe an MLP and its gradient-descent update;
explain the roles of seeds and repeated training runs in reproducibility and fair comparisons of model architectures;
interpret box plots, residual plots, error histograms, and feature-space heat maps, distinguishing observed errors and training variability from predictive uncertainty.
